            <ArticleTitle>Validating a research ethnicity questionnaire using genomic markers.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1649-1657</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pgs-2017-0051</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Population stratification is a confounding factor in genetic association studies. Self-report measures, the most common method of collecting ethnicity, may be less reliable for psychiatric patients. This study aims to validate our research ethnicity questionnaire as a reliable measure of genetic ancestry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The validity of our questionnaire was compared with genetic ancestry according to structured association tests and dimensional reduction methods. Our research tool was also compared with a standard multiple choice questionnaire.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Our research questionnaire was highly consistent with genetic ancestry. The standard questionnaire demonstrated a greater degree of inconsistency in identifying ethnicity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Collecting information on the geographical ancestry of each individual's grandparents provides a more comprehensive view of ethnicity to prevent population stratification and wasted finances on genotyping.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
